README for the Aurora LTFS messaging architecture

                ----------------------------------

Background
=============================

Aurora LTFS uses the ICU resource bundle mechanism to generate messages. Each
message generated by the system has a unique message ID that carries a severity
(Error, Warning, Information, Debug). The Aurora LTFS utilities print E, W, and
I messages by default.

Each subdirectory under the "messages" directory is one component and provides
the message bundle(s) for a single shared library or command. Every bundle
declares its own ID prefix in the "prefix:string" entry of its root table.


Message ID format
=============================

A message ID is an 8-character string:

    A <component code: 2 letters> <number: 4 digits> <severity: 1 letter>

    e.g. AFS0001I, ALC0002E, AED0098E

  * "A"        - fixed leading letter (Aurora).
  * Component  - two letters identifying the bundle that defines the message
                 (see the table below).
  * Number     - four characters, unique per prefix. Messages added on HEAD
                 use four digits; messages added on release branches use
                 letter-bearing shapes (see "Release-branch ID shapes"). The
                 same number may appear under different prefixes (AFS0001 and
                 ACK0001 coexist), but within one prefix a number is used at
                 most once across all severities: once AFS0001I exists,
                 AFS0001E/W/D must never be used.
  * Severity   - E (Error), W (Warning), I (Information), or D (Debug).


Release-branch ID shapes
=============================

To let release branches add messages in parallel with HEAD without any
cross-branch coordination, the shape of the 4-character number field encodes
the release line ("pool") that introduced the message. The three shapes are
mutually exclusive, so concurrently active lines draw from disjoint pools and
cannot collide by construction.

    Pool (where the ID was added)   Shape                     Example
    -----------------------------   -----------------------   -----------------
    HEAD (trunk)                    4 digits                  AFS0001E
    M.0.x patch series              Major letter + 3 digits   1.0.x -> AFSA001E
    M.m.x patch series (m >= 1)     Major letter + Minor      1.1.x -> AFSAA01E
                                    letter + 2 digits         2.1.x -> AFSBA01E

  * Version mapping: Major M maps to letter chr('A' + M - 1) (1 -> A,
    2 -> B); Minor m likewise (1 -> A). Major/Minor versions above 26 are
    out of scope.
  * Only one patch stream may be active per minor at a time; this serializes
    each pool's counter.
  * Majors are released directly from HEAD (no stabilization branch), so
    X.0.0 releases contain only digit-form IDs.
  * Backports keep their original ID; the pools are disjoint, so a trunk ID
    carried into a patch release cannot collide. A backported trunk ID is
    frozen as soon as any release ships it, even if HEAD itself has not
    released it.
  * AEI/AED are exempt: their numbers are derived from the LTFS_*/EDEV_*
    constants (see "Internal error codes"), which cannot encode letters.
    New error constants are added on HEAD only; release branches backport
    them.
  * All existing digit-form IDs form the trunk pool baseline; no renumbering
    is needed. validate_error_messages.py currently accepts only the digit
    form; widen its pattern to A[A-Z]{2}(\d{4}|[A-Z]\d{3}|[A-Z]{2}\d{2})[EIWD]
    when the first release branch is cut.

See GitHub issue #46 for the background discussion.


Component codes
=============================

The component code maps to the "messages" subdirectory that defines it
(1 directory = 1 component). Most components have a single prefix; the
libaltfs directory is split into eight bundles (lb, lc, lf, lg, li, lj, lp,
lx files) and the internal_error directory into two (ed, ei), each bundle
with its own prefix.

    messages/ subdir       prefix(es)
    --------------------   ----------------------------------------
    bin_altfs              AFS
    bin_altfsck            ACK
    bin_altfsindextool     AIX
    bin_mkaltfs            AMK
    internal_error         AED, AEI  (see "Internal error codes")
    iosched_fcfs           AIF
    iosched_unified        AIU
    kmi_flatfile           AKF
    kmi_simple             AKS
    libaltfs               ALB, ALC, ALF, ALG, ALI, ALJ, ALP, ALX
    tape_common            ATM
    tape_freebsd_cam       ATC
    tape_generic_file      ATF
    tape_generic_itdtimg   ATI
    tape_iokit             ATK
    tape_linux_sg          ATG

The second letter of the component code encodes the category:

    E = internal error registry
    I = I/O scheduler plugins
    K = key management (KMI) plugins
    L = libaltfs
    T = tape drive plugins

The command binaries use mnemonic codes instead: AFS = altfs, ACK = altfsck,
AIX = altfsindextool, AMK = mkaltfs.


Internal error codes (AEI / AED)
=============================

The internal_error component is a registry of the internal error codes defined
in src/libltfs/ltfs_error.h, so its numbers are derived from the constants
rather than allocated consecutively:

  * AEI - the number equals the value of the LTFS_* constant.
          e.g. LTFS_NULL_ARG (1000) -> AEI1000E
  * AED - the number equals the value of the EDEV_* constant minus 20000.
          e.g. EDEV_CLEANING_REQUIRED (20098) -> AED0098E


Message ID rules
=============================

The lifecycle rules for message IDs are maintained in the "Message ID Rules"
section of AGENTS.md at the repository root — refer to it instead of this
file for the authoritative wording. In short: keep numbers consecutive within
a prefix during development, never reuse a number once it has appeared in a
release, and comment out retired IDs with an "unused" prefix to reserve them.

Two checks keep the catalogs consistent:

  * validate_error_messages.py (repository root) cross-checks the IDs
    referenced in src/**/*.{c,h} against the IDs defined in the bundles and
    reports unused or undefined message IDs.
  * The "message-checker" CI build (--enable-message-checker, see
    .github/workflows/build.yml) expands ltfsmsg() against the catalog text
    so that gcc's -Wformat verifies the printf arguments of every call site
    at compile time.


Build plumbing
=============================

Refer to the following files and directories to see how a plugin provides
its messages to libaltfs.
  * messages/Makefile.am, messages/make_message_src.sh
        These files implement a system for compiling message bundle objects.
        Run make_message_src.sh (via make) to regenerate the bundle objects
        after changing a catalog.
  * messages/tape_generic_file
        This directory implements messages for the sample "file" backend
        (prefix ATF).
  * src/tape_drivers/generic/file/Makefile.am
        This file has DEPENDENCIES and LIBADD lines that link the compiled
        message bundle into the final plugin.
  * src/tape_drivers/generic/file/filedebug_tc.c
        This file implements a function to initialize the message bundle and
        tell libaltfs the name of the bundle.
  * messages/print_error_messages.py
        Utility that dumps the message catalogs.
